# A MicrochannelâConfined Crystallization Strategy Enables Blade Coating of Perovskite Single Crystal Arrays for Device Integration

## abstract
Perovskite single crystals (PSCs) possess superior optoelectronic properties compared to their corresponding polycrystalline films, but their applications of PSCs in high-performance, integrated devices are hindered by their heavy thickness and difficulty in scalable deposition. Here, a microchannel-confined crystallization (MCC) strategy to grow uniform and large-area PSC arrays for integrated device applications is reported. Benefiting from the confinement effect of the microchannels, solution flow dynamics is well controlled, and thus uniform deposition of PSC arrays with suitable thickness is achieved, meaning they are applicable for scale-up device applications. The resulting PSCs possess excellent optoelectronic properties in terms of a long carrier lifetime (175 ns) and an ultralow defect density (2 × 109 cm-3 ), which are comparable to the corresponding bulk crystals. The unique embedded structure of PSCs within the microchannels allows the construction of a high-integration image sensor. This work paves the way toward high-throughput growth of PSCs for integrated optoelectronic devices.

A Microchannel-Confined Crystallization Strategy Enables Blade Coating of Perovskite Single Crystal Arrays for Device Integration
Perovskite single crystals (PSCs) possess superior optoelectronic properties compared to their corresponding polycrystalline films, but their applications of PSCs in high-performance, integrated devices are hindered by their heavy thickness and difficulty in scalable deposition. Here, a microchannel-confined crystallization (MCC) strategy to grow uniform and large-area PSC arrays for integrated device applications is reported. Benefiting from the confinement effect of the microchannels, solution flow dynamics is well controlled, and thus uniform deposition of PSC arrays with suitable thickness is achieved, meaning they are applicable for scale-up device applications. The resulting PSCs possess excellent optoelectronic properties in terms of a long carrier lifetime (175 ns) and an ultralow defect density $(2\times10^{9}\mathsf{c m}^{-3})$ , which are comparable to the corresponding bulk crystals. The unique embedded structure of PSCs within the microchannels allows the construction of a highintegration image sensor. This work paves the way toward high-throughput growth of PSCs for integrated optoelectronic devices.
Organic−inorganic hybrid perovskites $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}P b X_{3}}$ , $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{l}}$ , Br, or I) have emerged as a new generation of solution-processable and inexpensive materials for various optoelectronic devices.[1−4] Most of these devices are made from the perovskite polycrystalline films, which inevitably contain a high density of structural defects and grain boundaries.[5] Perovskite single crystals (PSCs) with few charge traps possess superior optoelectronic properties in terms of higher carrier mobility and longer carrier diffusion length and lifetime.[6−10] With these improved properties, PSCs have promising potential in high-performance and integrated optoelectronic devices. For the growth of the PSCs, mainly three methods had been reported so far: solution supersaturating at reduced temperature, inverse solubility crystallization, and antisolvent vapor-assisted crystallization.[11−16] These methods tend to obtain bulk single crystals with thickness up to millimeter scale, which is much larger than the carrier diffuse length in PSCs and thus greatly hinders their device applications. To solve this problem, a space-confined strategy was introduced into these methods to grow PSCs with thickness from several hundreds of nanometers to a few micrometers.[14,17−19] However, the lateral dimension of the resulting PSCs was limited by lack of solution supplement at the confined areas to provide enough solutes for continuous crystal growth. Moreover, the reported growth techniques for PSCs are quite time consuming and batch processes with limited throughput.
Among various solution-based methods, blade coating has the advantages of high throughput, roll-to-roll compatibility, and minimal material wastage.[20−22]
Although it has been widely applied to fabricate perovskite thin films for device applications,[23−26] PSCs deposited by the blade coating method generally exhibit a nonuniform morphology with low crystallinity, making them very challenging to fabricate high-performance integrated devices. The solution fluidic flow instabilities are believed to be the primary cause.[25,27] During the coating process, perovskite solutes would be turbulently transported to various locations of meniscus front, and this yields perovskite supersaturated phase containing randomly aggregated solutes, resulting in inhomogeneous nucleation and formation of the nonuniform and misaligned seed crystals. Therefore, it still remains a great challenge to achieve large-area growth of PSCs with highly uniform morphology via blade coating.
Herein, we report the development, for the first time, of a microchannel-confined crystallization (MCC) strategy for the large-area growth of highly aligned PSCs with uniform morphology, which is applicable to device integration. The microchannels not only helped stabilize the transport of perovskite solutes but also reduced the density of nucleation events, ensuring the formation of uniform and continuous PSCs arrays in the channels. Both in situ experimental observation and theoretical ­simulation verified the vital roles of the microchannel in controlling the solution flow dynamics. The resulting PSCs possess a long carrier lifetime of 175 ns and an ultralow defect density of $2\times10^{9}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , which are comparable to the ­corresponding bulk single crystals. The unique embedded structure of PSCs within photoresist channels makes it possible to construct vertically stacked devices for real-world applications. As such, $10~\times~10$ addressable photodetector array was further demonstrated to achieve an excellent image functionality. Our approach opens opportunities for the large-scale fabrication of highly aligned, uniform PSCs for high-performance, integrated optoelectronic devices.
Figure 1a illustrates the blade-coating process of PSC arrays using the MCC strategy. First, an indium tin oxide (ITO)−glass substrate (typical $1.5\times1.5~\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) was patterned by photolitho­ graphy technique to form periodic PR microchannels $(2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ width and $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ depth, see Figure S1, Supporting Information). When perovskite solution was loaded into the connection area between the microchannels and the blade, some solution would flow out from the bulk solution and filled into the microchannels due to capillary action. The horizontal distance between the solution front in the microchannels and the bulk solution is about ${\approx}100~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Then, a blade dragged the perovskite solution across the microchannel-covered substrate at a temperature of ${\approx}35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . With solvent evaporation, the perovskite molecules would precipitate out within the microchannels and form seed crystals. Under an optimal coating speed of $30\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , the seed crystals could grow continuously, generating PSC arrays within the microchannels. A real-time observation by crosspolarized optical microscope (CPOM) is shown in Figure  1a and movie S1, Supporting Information, revealing the growth process of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ PSC arrays. Figure  1b shows the
CPOM image of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ PSC arrays at $45^{\circ}$ rotation angle with respect to the axis of the crossed polarizers. The colored regions are the PSCs while the black lines represent the PR stripes. The PSCs are neatly arranged in the array and they uniformly cover the entire ITO substrate. The occasional color difference in the CPOM image is owing to the small thickness variation of the PSCs. The detailed geometry and morphology of the PSCs were investigated by using scanning electron microscope (SEM). It indicates that all of the microchannels are filled with the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ crystals with an identical width of $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Figure  1c,d). The cross-sectional image shows the PSC with a trapezoid shape, which is in good accordance with the shape of the channels (Figure 1e). The SEM image also exhibits no observable defects and grain boundaries in the perovskite crystals. Atom force microscopy (AFM) image (Figure  1f) reveals that the perovskite crystal has a very smooth surface with a roughness of $4{\mathrm{-}}6~\mathrm{nm}$ . All these results indicate that we have obtained high-quality perovskite crystals in a large area. Moreover, the MCC method shows a good universality and is applicable to other organolead halide perovskites, such as methylammonium lead bromide $(\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{Br}_{3})$ and formamidinium lead iodide $(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})$ , for the growth of PSC arrays (Figure S2, Supporting Information). It is also worth noting that compared with the reported growth approaches for the perovskite crystal arrays,[9,10,18,19,28] the MCC method enables the blade coating of uniform and high-quality PSC arrays over large areas.
Crystals will demonstrate strong light reflectance under CPOM investigation due to their optical birefringence.[29,30]
Figure 2a displays the CPOM images of the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ PSC arrays at different rotation angles with respect to the axis of the crossed polarizers. It shows that each $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ crystal has a uniform luminous intensity. Moreover, when the PSCs are rotated relative to the polarizers’ axes, the luminous intensity changes continuously from bright to dark. Also, the variation tendency of the luminous intensity presents a fourfold symmetry (Figure  2b), indicating that the resultant perovskite crystals have a high anisotropy. To further evaluate the crystal quality of the PSCs, we carried out X-ray diffraction (XRD) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) investigations. XRD spectrum in Figure 2c displays two dominant diffraction peaks at $14.1^{\circ}$ and $28.3^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (110) and (220) planes, respectively. Note that the diffraction peaks of other planes don’t appear in the XRD pattern. This result confirms that the PSCs have a preferential orientation of [110] in the normal direction of the substrate. In a sharp contrast, the bladecoated perovskite crystals without using microchannels produce many diffraction peaks in the XRD spectrum (Figure  2c and Figure S3, Supporting Information), meaning that crystals with misaligned planes grow along the surface normal direction. Further, the PSCs on the ITO/glass substrate were transferred onto a $\mathtt{C u}$ grid for TEM characterization. The lowresolution TEM image reveals that the crystal is compact with a smooth surface (Figure  2d). The selective-area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns collected from three different regions in one perovskite crystal all display very sharp diffraction spots (Figure 2e), and the same diffraction patterns are observed for the SAED patterns acquired from different perovskite crystals (Figure S4, Supporting Information), confirming the singlecrystalline nature of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ crystals. In addition, the crystal growth direction of the PSCs can be determined to be [001] based on the SAED patterns. High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Figure 2f) reveals clear lattice fringes with a lattice spacing of $0.33\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which could be indexed as (004) of the tetragonal $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ phase. Besides, the energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis shows that I, N, and $\mathrm{Pb}$ elements are homogeneously distributed in the single PSC (Figure S5, Supporting Information). All of these results collectively demonstrate that high-quality, PSC arrays were successfully fabricated by blade coating.
To gain deep insight into the role of the microchannels, we performed computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations (details can be found in the Experimental Section and
Supporting Information). The simulation result (Figure  3a) verifies that the solution in the front of the meniscus is thoroughly confined into the microchannels and the relatively deep channels can prevent the solution from overflowing. Concave meniscus would emerge by the Laplace pressure $(\Delta p)$ , which can be expressed with the equation below:
$$
\Delta p=\frac{2\gamma}{R}=\frac{2\gamma\cos\theta}{\mathrm{~W~}}
$$
where $\gamma$ is the surface tension of the solution, $R$ is the curvature radius of the meniscus within the microchannel, $\theta$ is the solution contact angle with the microchannel sidewall, and $W$ is the width of the microchannels. The sidewall of the microchannels is highly lyophilic with a perovskite solution contact angle of $5^{\circ}$ , the $W$ is $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and the $\gamma$ is $37.1~\mathrm{mN~m}^{-1}$ . Thus, the Laplace pressure would reach a high value of $3.7\times10^{4}$ Pa (Figure  3b). This will facilitate the formation of the meniscus with a small curvature radius of ${\approx}2~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ within the channels, as shown in Figure 3c. Since the solvent evaporation is the primary driving force for crystal nucleation, we further simulated the solvent evaporation process within the microchannels. The evaporation flux at the contact line is much larger than that in the remaining parts (Figure  3d) due to the fast solvent evaporation at the solution edge. The solvent evaporation will drive outward convective flow from the interior toward the solution edge. Simultaneously, the perovskite solutes will be transferred to the contact line, yielding a perovskite supersaturated phase. Therefore, nucleation of perovskite crystals preferentially occurs at these regions. Finite element simulation of the solution evaporation process within the microchannel (Figure  3d) shows that the color near the contact line is uniform and consistent. This means that the evaporation flux along the contact line is nearly the same, ensuring the subsequent uniform crystal nucleation at the entire contact line. In addition, simulation of fluid flow around the contact line (Figure  3e) clearly shows that the microchannel is effective in creating a stable and unidirectional outward convective flow for mass transport. This is especially important for acquiring homogeneous crystal nucleus at the early stage of crystal growth. On the other hand, since the short contact line can significantly reduce the density of nucleation events, fewer crystal nuclei will be formed. During the subsequent blade-coating process, the confined solution stably traverses across all the microchannels. Progressively epitaxial growth of crystals within the microchannels will occur, resulting in the formation of uniform PSCs.
In a striking contrast, the blade-coated perovskite crystals without using the microchannels have a large variation in orientations and morphologies (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Without the confinement of the microchannels, the solution in the meniscus front would randomly flow and spread over the entire substrate, producing a long contact line (Figure S6a, Supporting Information). During the solvent evaporation process, due to the lack of the confined effect for the fluid, the perovskite molecules would be turbulently transported to various locations of meniscus front (Figure S6b, Supporting Information). As a result, inhomogeneous nucleation would occur at the meniscus front, leading to the formation of the non-uniform and misaligned perovskite crystals. On the other hand, the confinement effect is closely related with the microchannel size. In control experiments, microchannels with different widths and depths were employed. First, the width of microchannels was varied from 2, to 3, 5 and $10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ at a fixed microchannel depth of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . SEM images in Figure S7, Supporting Information indicate that, with increasing the width of microchannels, pinholes and defects gradually appeared in the obtained perovskite crystals. The use of relatively narrow microchannels (with width of 2 or $3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ) was beneficial to improve the crystallinity of the crystals. Second, we fixed the width of the microchannels at $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ and then changed the depth of microchannels from 0.5, to 1 and $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The results were shown in Figure S8, Supporting Information. Note that, when the depth was increased to $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , many grain boundaries were generated in the crystals (Figure S8a, Supporting Information), because the thicker solution layer in the channels increased the density of nucleation events. However, if the depth was decreased to $500\mathrm{nm}$ or less, it would be difficult to confine the solution flow within the channels. As a result, a polycrystalline film would be formed on the template surface (Figure S8c, Supporting Information). Only at an appropriate depth of ${\approx}1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , high-quality PSCs could be obtained (Figure S8b, Supporting Information). Based on these results, it can be concluded that the optimal microchannel size in this study are with a depth of ${\approx}1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a width of ${\approx}2~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .
The blade-coating speed determines the meniscus shape within the microchannels, which will influence the morphology and crystal quality of the materials. Previous studies have shown that the optimal blade-coating speed $(V_{\mathrm{b}})$ should be close to the crystallization velocity $(V_{\mathrm{c}})$ of materials.[21] In order to extract the $V_{\mathrm{c}}$ of the PSCs in our study, we conducted the solution evaporation experiment without the blade-coating and plotted the contact line position versus time in Figure S9, Supporting Information on the basis of optical microscope investigation on the growth process of the PSCs (Movie S1, Supporting Information). The movement of the contact line with time represents the crystallization velocity of PSCs. A linear relation between the contact line position and time could be observed, giving rise to an equilibrium speed $V_{\mathrm{c}}$ of $32\upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . Therefore, if the $V_{\mathrm{b}}$ was far below $32\upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (such as $15\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ), the contact line would retract faster than the solution motion. Due to the capillary action, the solution within the microchannels was maintained in a constant amount. This would lead to the ­solution aggregation within the microchannels, thereby generating thick and rough crystals (Figure S10a, Supporting Information). At a $V_{\mathrm{b}}$ much larger than $32~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (such as $50\upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ , the confined solution within the microchannels has not fully evaporated after the blade across the channels, leading to a non-uniform crystallization due to the “coffee-ring” effect (Figure S10c, Supporting Information). Clearly, only the PSCs produced at $V_{\mathrm{b}}\approx V_{\mathrm{c}}$ presented the uniform morphology and high crystallinity (Figure S10b, Supporting Information).
Further, the optical and electrical properties of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ PSC arrays were investigated. The PSC arrays exhibit an absorption cutoff at ${\approx}800$ nm and a photoluminescence (PL) emission peak at ${\approx}777$ nm (Figure  4a). Accordingly, the optical bandgap of PSCs could be extracted to be $1.53\ \mathrm{eV}.$ This value is matched with prior measurements on the perovskite thin films.[31] The PL peak value is smaller than the absorption onset, implying the PL emission could be extinguished by themselves, consistent with the result of the bulk single crystals.[7] The blueshifted and narrow PL peak also indicate a lower trap density in the PSCs.[13] The PL decay curve of the PSC arrays in Figure  4b can be described by biexponential fitting, giving a short-lived PL lifetime of 24  ns with a nonradiative percentage of $13.7\%$ and a long-lived PL lifetime of 151 ns with a radiative percentage of $86.3\%$ . It should be noted that the proportion of nonradiative recombination is much lower than that of the radiative recombination, suggesting that the PSC arrays have very few surface traps. The average carrier lifetime of the PSC arrays is determined to be ${\approx}175$ ns. In comparison, the blade-coated perovskite crystals without using the microchannels exhibit a much shorter carrier lifetime of ${\approx}65$ ns, manifesting a higher trap-state density in the sample. Scanning photocurrent (PC) mapping of the PSC array-based lateral device reveals consistent PCs over the entire crystals (Figure  4c,d). This double confirms that the obtained PSCs have few trap states. To quantitatively analyze the trap-state density in the PSCs, we fabricated a hole-only device by connecting the PSCs between two Au electrodes.[13,32] The channel length of the device is about $4.4\ \mathrm{mm}$ . We measured the current−voltage trace for the device and found three regions in the curve as shown in Figure 4e. The trap-state density $(n_{\mathrm{traps}})$ can be determined to be ${\approx}2\times10^{9}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ by the trap-filled limit (TFL) region. The $n_{\mathrm{traps}}$ value is comparable with the best bulk $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ single crystals reported to date,[13] and is almost three orders of magnitude lower than that of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ polycrystalline thin film.[19,32] The above results show that the PSC arrays fabricated using the MCC strategy have outstanding optical and electrical properties, which is very critical for their high-performance optoelectronic device applications.
Compared with the lateral-type perovskite photodetectors, the vertical-type devices with short channel length could not only show high-current characteristics under relatively ­low-voltage conditions but also could simplify the route of the device integration.[33] Unfortunately, there are very few studies on the PSCs for the vertical-structure photodetectors, possibly due to the difficulties in the construction of compacted PSC crystals in the vertical structures. In this work, the unique embedded structure of PSCs within the microchannels allows the construction of PSC array-based vertically stacked devices, and enables the implementation of the photodetection circuitry for image sensors. Moreover, the embedded structure can also protect the PSCs against the invasion of water and oxygen, which will be conductive to improve the device stability.[28,34] Figure  5a illustrates device structure of the image sensor. The image sensor consists of the glass supporting substrate, ITO bottom electrodes, PSC arrays embedded in the microchannels, and $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Au}$ top electrodes. Cross-sectional SEM image of a single pixel of the image sensor can be seen in Figure  5b, showing a vertically stacked device structure. Figure  5c depicts the typical current−voltage $\left(I-V\right)$ curves of a single pixel measured in the dark and under the illumination of $100~\upmu\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ green light $(532\mathrm{nm})$ . The green light triggers a significant increase in the device current, which is 3–4 orders of magnitude higher than the dark current. The $I{-}V$ curves exhibit a significantly asymmetric feature, suggesting that the device is a typical Schottky-type photodetector. The principle of the Schottky-type device can be understood by plotting the energy-band diagram, as displayed in the insert in Figure  5c. The Fermi level $(E_{\mathrm{f}})$ of the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ $(4.67\ \mathrm{eV})$ is close to the work function of ITO (4.7 eV), so they can form an ohmic contact. In contrast, a high Schottky barrier will exist at the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ crystal $-\mathrm{MoO}_{3}\mathrm{-Au}$ interface because of the large difference of the $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ with the work function of $\mathtt{M o O}_{3}–\mathtt{A u}$ $(5.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ ) The schematic band alignments for forward and reverse biases and the PC rectification mechanism can be found in Figure S11, Supporting Information. As we know, the PC is in positive correlation with incidence light intensity. To quantitatively analyze the correlation, we performed light intensity-dependent PC measurements, indicating a linear relationship (Figure  5d). Accordingly, linear dynamic range (LDR) of the device is deduced to be 88  dB according to the following equation:
$$
\mathrm{LDR}=20\mathrm{log}\frac{J_{\mathrm{upper}}\left(L_{\mathrm{upper}}\right)}{J_{\mathrm{lower}}\left(L_{\mathrm{lower}}\right)}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{R=\frac{J_{\mathrm{ph}}-J_{\mathrm{dark}}}{L_{\mathrm{light}}}}\\ {D^{\ast}{=}\frac{\left(\mathrm{A}f\right)^{0.5}}{\left(i_{\mathrm{n}}/R\right)}}\end{array}
$$
In summary, due to complex solution dynamics presented in blade-coating process, scale-up deposition of uniform and high-quality PSCs always remains challenging. Here, we demonstrated that the MCC strategy enables the high-throughput fabrication of high-quality PSCs by blade coating, which can be compatible with industrial-level high-throughput roll-to-roll manufacturing. In this strategy, microscale PR channels were introduced to constrain inner fluid flow and define the size of contact line, which could effectively stabilize mass transport and reduce nucleation events at early stage of crystal growth. With solvent evaporation, homogeneous nucleation occurred at the contact line and high-quality PSCs would form within the channels. After that, long-range epitaxial growth was achieved by continuous solute transport via blade coating, forming the PSC arrays over large area. The roles of the microscale PR channels on stabilizing fluid and reducing nucleation events were verified through in situ microscopy observations and quantitative CFD simulations. The resulting PSCs showed high crystal quality and very low $n_{\mathrm{trap}}$ of $2\times10^{9}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ comparable to the bulk PSCs. The availability of the large-area fabrication of PSC arrays made it possible to construct $10\times10$ addressable photodetector array for image sensor application, which successfully demonstrated a clear and high-resolution imaging capability. Our method opens a possible way for utilization of PSCs toward the high-performance integrated optoelectronic devices.
## Experimental Section
Materials: ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ was purchased from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp (http://www.p-oled.cn). Anhydrous DFM was purchased from Sigma−Aldrich. All materials were used without further purification.
Preparation of the Microchannels: First, ITO glass substrates were cleaned using an ultrasonicator in isopropanol, acetone, and deionized water bath for $\mathsf{10}\mathsf{m i n}$ successively and then subjected to oxygen−plasma treatment for 15 min. Next, UV photolithography on the clean ITO glass substrates was performed by a mask aligner (Karl Suss, MJB4) using SU-8 photoresist (MicroChem 2000.5) to fabricate the SU-8 PR strips with the same intervals. After that, the ITO glass substrates were washed with ethanol, dried in a stream of ${\sf N}_{2}$ , and treated with ultraviolet−ozone for $15\mathrm{\min}$ to remove contaminants.
Growth and Characterization of PSC Arrays: A ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ solution was first prepared by dissolving ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ $(0.003~{\ m o l})_{,}$ ) in DMF $(70m L)$ ). The ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ solution $(70-20~\upmu\up L)$ was dropped on the microchannels, which were set on a hot plate at $35^{\circ}C,$ , and then scraped horizontally by a microdisplacement platform at a certain speed. The gap between blade and substrate was controlled to be $\approx150\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . After coating, the PSC arrays were annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10\min$ . All experiments were carried out under ambient conditions. The ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ single crystal arrays were characterized using a cross-polarized optical microscopy (Olympus, BX51), a SEM (Carl Zeiss, Supra 55) with an accelerating voltage of $\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{l}0\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{k}\mathsf{V},$ and a scanning probe microscope (Asylum Research, Cypher S). The crystallinity and elemental analysis of the ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ single crystals were determined via SAED and EDX spectrometry with a TEM (FEI, Tecnai G2 F20) at an accelerating voltage of $200~\mathsf{k V}.$ The large-area crystallinity was measured by an XRD (Empyrean, PANalytical B.V.) using a Cu $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ source. The optical properties of the ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ single crystal arrays were measured using an ultraviolet− visible spectrophotometer (PerkinElmer, model Lambda 750), a fluorescence spectrometer (Varian, FLS920P), and a transient state fluorescence spectrometer (HORIBA Jobin Yvon, FL-TCSPC).
Measurement of Scanning Photocurrent Mapping of the PSC ArrayBased Lateral Device: A home-built scanning PC microscopy composed of an optical system and a semiconductor parameter analyzer was used to measure the PC as a function of local photoinjection position. In the optical system, a $500~\mathsf{n m}$ laser was brought into a $700\times$ zoom lens through a multimode fiber to produce a ${\approx}2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ laser spot. The laser spot was projected onto the surface of PSC and then scanned in the xy-plane. Finally, the PC was recorded as a function of the laser location.
Computational Fluid Dynamics Simulations: Commercial software package ANSYS DesignModeler was used for the geometry construction, and Meshing was used for mesh generation. Fluent and CFD-Post was used for calculation and data visualization, respectively. This process was simplified as a laminar two-phase flow of air and liquid. Volume of fluid (VOF) model with wall adhesion effects was employed to trace the air− liquid interface. The surface tension for DMF was set at $35.8~\mathsf{m N}~\mathsf{m}^{-1}$ , the contact angle was set at $5^{\circ}$ and $20^{\circ}$ for the side walls and bottom sides of the microchannels, respectively. The evaporation simulation in the microchannels was carried out using COMSOL (details can be found in Supporting Information).
Fabrication and Characterization of PSC Array-based Photodetectors and Image Sensor: Microchannels were fabricated on a glass substrate that was prepatterned by ITO electrodes with a typical width of $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Then microchannels were fabricated on the prepatterned ITO electrodes by UV photolithography using SU-8 PR and PSC arrays were grown on the microchannels. Afterward, $20\ n m$ -thick ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}$ and $100\ n m$ -thick Au finger electrodes were evaporated successively on the PSC arrays using the same stainless-steel shadow mask. The photodetectors were measured by using a semiconductor characterization system (Keithley, S4200) on a probe station (EverBeing, PE4). A xenon lamp coupled to a monochromator (Zolix, Omni-λBright 300) was used as the light source to provide monochromatic light that illuminated the photodetector in normal direction. The light intensity on the device was regulated by varying the slit width of the monochromator, which was calibrated with a silicon photodiode (Newport, 918D-UV-OD3R). To demonstrate imaging function, the imaged character $"\mathsf{A}"$ was projected onto the $10\times$ 10 addressable photodetector array. A convex lens was used to focus the pattern.
